#Decoupling Guessing From Asking

By this point, the environment had already been through several rounds of surgery.

The action space had been rebalanced;

The observation had settled on dynamic split ratios;

The reward had moved from linear elimination to information gain;

Training and evaluation had been split into two separate horizons;

On paper, the experiment looked done.

Then I actually played against it;


# What I Noticed by Playing, Not by Reading Metrics

Win rate and average steps had told me the agent was learning to do something, but I couldn't exactly figure what and how.

Playing a live game answered my questions;

The agent asked reasonable early questions.
Sex, region, hair color, industry.
The candidate pool dropped in a way that looked like real deduction.

But near the end of one game, something stood out:

> Step 1: Is Sex = female? → No (1811 remaining)

> Step 19: Is Sex = male? → Yes (still 2 remaining)

Eighteen steps after learning the secret wasn't female, the agent spent one of its last two turns re-confirming that it was male.

Nothing changed;

No candidates were eliminated;

It had already known the answer implicitly since step 1, but had no way of representing that.

It ran out of turns one step short of separating the last two candidates, and guessed wrong.

That single transcript exposed two separate problems I had been treating as one.

# Problem One: The agent wasn't tracking what it already knew.

The asked mask only recorded literal (feature, value) pairs.

"Sex = female" and "Sex = male" were two different entries in that list.

Answering one told the agent nothing about the other, even though logically, once a feature's value is implied for every remaining candidate, asking about it again cannot produce new information.

The environment had no concept of a "resolved feature" only a flat history of exact questions asked.

# Problem Two: The agent couldn't actually win by guess

This one took longer to see, because it wasn't a bug so much as a structural mismatch I had built in from the start.

The whole point of the split-ratio representation was to keep the agent blind to candidate features, so it would learn a transferable questioning strategy rather than memorizing the dataset;

That part worked as intended.

But the action space still included a guess action for every individual candidate,  and the reward still gave +10 for guessing correctly.

That meant the agent was being asked to solve an identity dependent problem using an observation deliberately designed to contain no identity information;

It structurally could not learn that part, no matter how much training it received.

The agent had never been able to win in the sense of identifying the right guess;

because the representation that made asking learnable was the same one that made guessing unlearnable.

# Reframing the Win Condition

Holding the assumption that the characters in this dataset are unique,

If no two candidates share every feature value;
then "the pool has narrowed to one candidate" and "every feature of the secret is now known" are the same event.

Guessing isn't a separate skill sitting at the end of a good game, it's just the label we put on what elimination already accomplished;

There was nothing left for a learnt action to decide.

That reframing dissolved both problems from the same root fix.

# The Fix

Guessing came out of the action space entirely:

The environment now only exposes question actions.

There is no guess-action dimension and no premature-guess penalty to enforce.

Resolved features get folded into the asked_mask;

After every step, the environment checks each feature:

If the active candidate pool has converged on a single value for it;

-whether that came from directly asking, or purely as fallout from other eliminations;
every question tied to that feature is marked asked, even the ones never literally asked.


The win condition stayed exactly where it already was:

The pool narrowing to one candidate — but is now understood as the only condition, not one path among several.

Guessing itself moved out of the learned policy and into the game logic;

once elimination hits a singleton, there's nothing left to decide-just an answer to report.

# What This Changed in the Experiment

The action space shrank significantly:

From thousands of guess-actions plus a handful of questions, down to just the questions.

That alone should make exploration far more tractable than it ever was in the original design.

More importantly, the reward signal is now fully attributable to the thing I actually wanted to measure:

how well the agent asks.

